// TEST: Match-bodied function contradicts antisymm (rejects)
// ERROR: BL4013
// The TExprMatch rule, asserted the same way the binding-form descent is: a
// declared clause the body now disproves. Every part of this match is
// invariant under the (x, y) swap —
//   * scrutinee `x + y`: the two children ARE each other's image, and
//     opSwapClass OpAdd is PInv;
//   * guard `s > 0.0`: `s` is bound BY the pattern, and an invariant scrutinee
//     selects the same arm and decomposes to the same value under the swap, so
//     a pattern binder is invariant with no substitution needed — that is the
//     load-bearing argument for the whole rule;
//   * both arms: `x * y` mirrors under OpMul (PInv), and `x * y + 1.0` is
//     PInv + PInv.
// Arms agreeing on PInv make the match PInv, which contradicts the declared
// antisymm. Before the match rule the whole node was PBottom and this file
// compiled.
//
// The match stays in a NAMED FUNCTION body, never under a loop kernel: there
// are no kernel-position matches anywhere in the corpus and Grad refuses them
// in differentiated code, so that combination is unexercised territory that
// this rule does not need.
function pick(x, y) where anticomm(x, y) = match x + y with
    | s if s > 0.0 -> x * y
    | _ -> x * y + 1.0
let bad = pick(1.0, 2.0)
